WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.370
DANIEL GARRAHAN: Street food at
covered markets in coronavirus.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.180
It doesn't sound like
a recipe for success.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.640
TIM HAYWARD: We've come to
South London's Brixton Village

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.142
to see how the restaurants
here are coping.

00:00:13.142 --> 00:00:15.350
DANIEL GARRAHAN: Small
independents run tight margins

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.480
at the best of times,
so how did they

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.090
respond when the pandemic hit?

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.217
TIM HAYWARD: How can they
survive a post-lockdown future?

00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:26.800
DANIEL GARRAHAN: Tim, we've done
the Michelin star restaurant.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.938
We've seen the local
neighbourhood restaurant.

00:00:28.938 --> 00:00:30.730
We're here at Brixton
Village Market today.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.980
Quite different scale to the
places we've been so far.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.730
What's it going to be
like for these guys?

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:36.190
It must be really challenging.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.500
TIM HAYWARD: It's going to
be pretty awful for them.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.590
This isn't just a matter
of getting balance.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:46.120
Small, independent operations
are absolutely the majority,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.460
the backbone of this sector.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.380
And the other thing
that most civilians

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.975
don't get, to be honest, is
how tight their margins are.

00:00:53.975 --> 00:00:56.350
Everybody in the restaurant
industry-- yeah, your margins

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:56.850
are tight.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:57.510
That's fine.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.350
That's great when you've
got a GBP 6,000,000

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.950
operation in West End.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.395
These guys' margins are--

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:03.783
we're really talking pennies.

00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:05.950
DANIEL GARRAHAN: But it'll
be interesting to see now

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.987
whether those people who
are stuck at home, working

00:01:08.987 --> 00:01:10.570
from home for the
foreseeable future--

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.800
whether they're going to
come to the rescue for a lot

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.020
these small, independent guys.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.550
TIM HAYWARD: And as we're
proving with everything

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.950
we've done so far, it's
about the relationship

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:18.940
to the local community.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:19.310
DANIEL GARRAHAN: All right.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:19.852
Let's go see.

00:01:19.852 --> 00:01:22.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.305
The market's general manager,
Diana, shows us around.

00:01:25.305 --> 00:01:26.680
The restaurants
here rub shoulder

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.927
to shoulder with grocers,
butchers, fishmongers,

00:01:28.927 --> 00:01:29.510
and retailers.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.400
TIM HAYWARD: I've never
met a female fishmonger.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.858
DANIEL GARRAHAN: And they were
all given a three-month rent

00:01:33.858 --> 00:01:35.400
break during lockdown.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.150
TIM HAYWARD: We're
also interested to hear

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.980
that this place is
Grade II listed,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.170
meaning it has to
be preserved not

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:43.960
for its architectural beauty,
but for its cultural diversity.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.460
DIANA NABAGEREKA:
Our responsibility

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.420
as a custodian for the market
is to ensure that the retail mix

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.350
is retained.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.950
So there must always be
a Latin American butcher.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.590
There must always be a
Caribbean fishmonger.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:03.570
There must always be Esme, who
sells natural and organic herbs

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.650
that she ships over
from the Caribbean.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.277
So it's those sorts of tenants
that have to be preserved.

00:02:08.277 --> 00:02:10.360
TIM HAYWARD: So this is
written into your listing.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:10.594
DIANA NABAGEREKA: It's bizarre.

00:02:10.594 --> 00:02:11.810
TIM HAYWARD: There's
nothing you can do about it.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:11.980
DIANA NABAGEREKA: Sorry.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:12.580
I say bizarre.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.120
It's actually beautiful.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.930
I've never seen a listing
so splendid and bizarre.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.570
TIM HAYWARD: So what
if I'm a McDonald's?

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:20.710
Can I come?

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.270
DIANA NABAGEREKA: No, thank you.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.110
This space really cultivates
individuality and just

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.880
independent entrepreneurship.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.380
They get it wrong
a lot of the time,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.420
but through that
comes experiences

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.410
and just really, really
great, great tenant mixes.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:36.410
TIM HAYWARD: Hey, Brian.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:37.130
You all right?

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:38.505
DANIEL GARRAHAN:
Brian definitely

00:02:38.505 --> 00:02:39.970
has the entrepreneurial spirit.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.178
He says he was panicking
when the country locked down

00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:43.460
and the restaurant closed.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.710
But he's not only survived.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.120
He's thrived.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.120
TIM HAYWARD: The rent
break, [INAUDIBLE] scheme,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.100
and VAT reduction
were a big help.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.140
But he was ready to do anything.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.390
BRIAN DANCLAIR: For instance,
this is how I will plan.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.210
I bought a lawnmower,
just in case

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.330
I couldn't work any
further, work anymore,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:00.728
to go and cut grass.

00:03:00.728 --> 00:03:03.120
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.990
TIM HAYWARD: You put your face
on the sauce bottle, damn it.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:07.620
All this going on,
and yet, still,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.120
in the back of your mind,
there was a point in there

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.160
when you were thinking, I might
have to go mow people's lawns.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.170
BRIAN DANCLAIR: I have
to do anything that's

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.250
necessary to be relevant,
to make money for my family,

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.295
to feed my family, to pay my
mortgage, everything like that.

00:03:20.295 --> 00:03:21.275
You know what I mean?

00:03:21.275 --> 00:03:23.012
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:23.012 --> 00:03:24.720
DANIEL GARRAHAN:
Instead of mowing lawns,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.430
he's used social
media to stay in touch

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.640
with the local community, ramped
up takeaway, and streamlined

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:30.690
his menu to save costs.

00:03:33.453 --> 00:03:34.620
BRIAN DANCLAIR: Two things--

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.160
Brexit and, as well as the
COVID, the prices of some items

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.480
have gone up.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.210
So of course, we had to take
that component out and focus

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:49.525
on getting them just right,
to be on pace, to be focused.

00:03:49.525 --> 00:03:50.400
You know what I mean?

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.370
And that was the idea, and
that worked fantastic for us.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.548
I changed the prices a bit.

00:03:55.548 --> 00:03:57.090
The rum punch, for
instance-- we were

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:58.470
doing a big and a small one.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.970
We are doing just
the big ones now.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.460
Anytime you have
situations like this,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.000
something always
comes out of it,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.028
and you have to really be
positive and get it done.

00:04:08.028 --> 00:04:09.070
That's how we look at it.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.230
DANIEL GARRAHAN: So you look
at it as an opportunity.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:12.772
There's always
opportunity like this.

00:04:12.772 --> 00:04:16.500
You take this as
a learning curve.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.208
Improve yourself.

00:04:17.208 --> 00:04:18.750
TIM HAYWARD: They
have the resources,

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.740
and they have the agility
to actually do that.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.810
DANIEL GARRAHAN:
There's that word again.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:22.310
Agility.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.940
BRIAN DANCLAIR:
Agility's important.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.400
You have to be agile
in this business.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.400
If you can't manoeuvre, and
if you're just stagnant,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.840
you get beaten.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.860
So this is how we were able to
move tables around and change

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.637
the structure of the restaurant.

00:04:36.637 --> 00:04:38.970
If we had to change this
restaurant to takeaway tonight,

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:39.360
we could do it.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:39.960
Boom.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.490
Easily.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.010
TIM HAYWARD: If agility is
one word that comes up a lot,

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.870
community is another.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.580
DANIEL GARRAHAN:
Moto runs Okan, who's

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:52.320
been serving up down-to-earth
Japanese food for years.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.450
When COVID hit, she
didn't know what to do.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.440
TIM HAYWARD: What
happened when COVID hit?

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.860
MOTO PRIESTMAN: Oh, gosh, yeah.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:04.480
Completely stopped, and just
thinking, what should I do?

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.680
When should I reopen?

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.560
Should I open the
takeaway or not?

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:13.570
Or I need to save my
staff, keep safe, so I

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.010
shouldn't open takeaway, even?

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.040
And so just going around,
around, but there's no answer.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.040
DANIEL GARRAHAN: When the
restaurant did reopen,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.207
it was the community that
gave her the encouragement

00:05:23.207 --> 00:05:24.610
to keep going.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.590
MOTO PRIESTMAN: From the local
people saying, "How are you?"

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:28.630
And, "Are you open?"

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:29.920
"Well done."

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.380
These repeating,
repeating, hearing

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:38.320
the words and warm
feeling, I thought, OK.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.695
I think Okan udon
going to burst.

00:05:39.695 --> 00:05:41.362
DANIEL GARRAHAN: Sure
you're optimistic?

00:05:41.362 --> 00:05:42.485
MOTO PRIESTMAN: [LAUGHS]

00:05:42.485 --> 00:05:44.360
DANIEL GARRAHAN: As
optimistic as you can be.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.235
MOTO PRIESTMAN: Yeah.

00:05:45.235 --> 00:05:48.595
I think that's
always my character,

00:05:48.595 --> 00:05:53.440
but COVID-19 made
me, definitely,

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.815
very weak and nervous.

00:05:54.815 --> 00:05:56.440
TIM HAYWARD: Is it
fair to say you feel

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:57.755
supported by the community?

00:05:57.755 --> 00:05:58.630
MOTO PRIESTMAN: Yeah.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:02.590
Not necessarily, they
come to buy from us.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:06.160
Some people will buy from
us, get the dish from us.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.800
But some people--
I've seen them, maybe,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.250
over the last five, six
years, almost once a week

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:14.710
or once a month.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.777
We don't know names each
other, but we recognise faces.

00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:19.360
TIM HAYWARD: You're
saying community--

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.610
it affects your mental health.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:21.525
MOTO PRIESTMAN: Yeah.

00:06:21.525 --> 00:06:22.900
TIM HAYWARD: It
makes you strong.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.665
That's tremendous at that level.

00:06:25.665 --> 00:06:27.525
That is so exciting
to find that.

00:06:27.525 --> 00:06:28.900
But what I love
about restaurants

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.840
is the soul and the
emotion and the feeling

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.860
of being loved--
true hospitality.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.870
And what you're saying is
that that's being rewarded.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.265
Those are the ones who
are going to survive this.

00:06:40.265 --> 00:06:41.890
DANIEL GARRAHAN: The
fall in the market

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:44.670
has plummeted as much
as 90% during lockdown,

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.353
but it's started to return
to more normal levels.

00:06:48.353 --> 00:06:50.770
TIM HAYWARD: Diana says the
one positive from the pandemic

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:52.870
is how Britain's old
and new residents have

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.330
been brought together in the
same space at the same time.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:57.247
DIANA NABAGEREKA: I've
always seen this market

00:06:57.247 --> 00:07:01.350
as having an old Brixton and
a new Brixton that visit it.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.120
The old Brixton has
lived here for decades

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.070
and slowly started to
move out of Brixton,

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:08.140
perhaps living in
Croydon, living in Purley,

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.060
because they, perhaps, may
not be able to afford it.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.200
But they still come here to get
their produce and their goods.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.050
They come in in the morning.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.383
They use it as their community
space, and they enjoy it.

00:07:15.383 --> 00:07:16.800
Then they go home at 6 o'clock.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.540
New Brixton leaves
Brixton at 8:00 AM.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:20.545
They go into Victoria.

00:07:20.545 --> 00:07:21.670
They go into Oxford Circus.

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:23.110
They go to [INAUDIBLE]
Street and work, and then

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:24.290
come back at 6:00 PM.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.930
So when old Brixton
leaves, new Brixton

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.240
comes and then enjoys
our nighttime economy.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.720
What we learnt was, actually,
when new Brixton wasn't allowed

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:33.730
to go to work and
had to work at home,

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.700
they were then spending
their time shopping and using

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.230
the space like old Brixton.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:41.080
So there's now-- yeah, Henry and
Katie walk around the market,

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.862
where previously, it was
Patrice and Ronald, honestly.

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:45.070
Yeah, and I really love that.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:46.813
And they walk side by side now.

00:07:46.813 --> 00:07:48.730
MOTO PRIESTMAN: Oh,
you've got own chopsticks.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:49.470
TIM HAYWARD: Of course.

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:49.760
Thank you.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:50.760
MOTO PRIESTMAN: Amazing.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.562
I've never seen.

00:07:52.562 --> 00:07:53.500
Wow.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:54.910
DANIEL GARRAHAN: Well, first
thing I'm going to say to him

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:56.535
is, not only am I
impressed that you've

00:07:56.535 --> 00:07:58.420
got your face mask with you.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.720
You've also got
your own chopsticks.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.630
This is commitment to the
COVID security course.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.112
TIM HAYWARD: No,
it's just easier.

00:08:07.112 --> 00:08:09.070
I think more people should
carry their own kit.

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.653
DANIEL GARRAHAN: The
one thing I would

00:08:10.653 --> 00:08:13.750
say, having spoken to a few of
the food vendors in this place,

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:17.740
is how heartwarming it
is that most of them

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.478
are telling a really
positive story.

00:08:19.478 --> 00:08:21.520
They're obviously at the
sharp end of this thing,

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.730
but most of them seem,
actually, quite optimistic.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.035
TIM HAYWARD: So these guys
do have a positive outlook,

00:08:27.035 --> 00:08:29.160
because their landlord has
looked to them and said,

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:29.450
OK, now.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:30.690
We've got to keep
these people here.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:31.790
We've got to help them out.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:32.814
DANIEL GARRAHAN: They'd given
them this three-month break,

00:08:32.814 --> 00:08:33.260
of course.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.927
TIM HAYWARD: They've
given them a break.

00:08:34.927 --> 00:08:36.789
They will continue to
get the rent breaks.

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:41.950
They're already predisposed
to help small and diverse

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:43.770
businesses in the first place.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:48.010
And here, this is the definition
of what London community should

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:48.690
really be about.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:50.500
It's massively diverse.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.110
It's warm.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:51.910
It's friendly.

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:53.105
It's diverse by class.

00:08:53.105 --> 00:08:57.313
It's diverse by culture,
by ethnicity, and it works.

00:08:57.313 --> 00:08:58.480
DANIEL GARRAHAN: Well, look.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.460
If these noodles are
anything to go by,

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:02.880
I hope the community
does stick with it,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.340
and we get to enjoy
another bowl sometime soon.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:06.340
TIM HAYWARD: Absolutely.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.390
DANIEL GARRAHAN:
Mmm, that was good.

